  "estimatedSeconds": 630,
  "stem": "Let $F = \\dfrac{1 - \\tan^2\\theta}{1 + \\tan^2\\theta}$.",
  "parts": [
   {
    "label": "(a)",
    "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
    "marks": 4,
    "markingMode": "MANUAL",
    "stem": "Show that $F \\equiv 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "markScheme": {
      "points": [
       {
        "id": "p1",
        "description": "M1 replaces the denominator by $\\sec^2\\theta$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p2",
        "description": "M1 multiplies through by $\\cos^2\\theta$ (or writes $\\tan^2\\theta = \\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}$).",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p3",
        "description": "A1 obtains $\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta$.",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p4",
        "description": "A1 uses $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta$ to reach $2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$.",
        "marks": 1
       }
      ]
     },
     "modelAnswer": "$$\nF = \\frac{1 - \\tan^2\\theta}{\\sec^2\\theta} = \\cos^2\\theta\\,(1 - \\tan^2\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\cos^2\\theta - \\cos^2\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta} = \\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\cos^2\\theta - (1 - \\cos^2\\theta) = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1\n$$"
    },
    "explanation": "The denominator $1 + \\tan^2\\theta$ is $\\sec^2\\theta$, and dividing by $\\sec^2\\theta$ is multiplying by $\\cos^2\\theta$."
   },
